  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1021 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nClear skies and calming winds this evening allowing for\nsignificant radiational cooling. Temperatures are forecast to\nfall into the low 30s, below freezing for much of eastern\nNebraska and southwest Iowa. A freeze warning is in place for\nour area from 1AM to 9AM. With such rapid cooling with calm\nwinds we could see some patchy fog develop in low-lying areas\nand near bodies of water. Temperatures near or below freezing \ncould mean we see a few slick spots develop on bridges and \noverpasses.\n\nOvernight we're going to see the general wind direction shift \nfrom northerly to southerly as the surface high slides off to\nthe southeast and ridging builds in overhead. We won't see much\nwarming from this just yet on Saturday with highs only in the \n50s, but overnight lows Saturday night into early Sunday will \nonly fall into the upper 30s to low 40s. Going into Sunday a\nmore substantial southwesterly pressure gradient sets up across\nour area bringing in warm, dry air from the Desert Southwest.\nThis will warm temperatures into the mid 60s Sunday afternoon. \n\nA deepening trough slides down the West Coast early next week \nleading to amplified southwesterly flow pushing into the Four \nCorners region. Models continue to delay when this trough \nejects eastward toward our area, delaying precipitation chances,\nnow more likely Friday into Saturday. Temperatures will be \nwarming with highs warming into the 70s by Wednesday and near 80\nThursday and Friday.\n\nOne concern to monitor next week is the potential for fire\nweather concerns. With dry weather and warming temperatures,\nfuels will be drying out more through the week. Right now the\nwindiest period looks to be right as precipitation arrives on\nFriday, but any windier periods that may develop earlier in the\nweek could mean dangerous fire conditions.",
